{"name":"Chalcedony Violet","hex":"#6770ae","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chalcedony","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A form of fine-grained quartz that is nearly transparent or has a milky translucence; it fractures conchoidally."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chalcedony"},{"term":"violet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant or flower of the genus Viola, especially the fragrant Viola odorata;  similar-looking plants and flowers.","A person thought to resemble V. odorata, especially in its beauty and delicacy."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a violet colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/violet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chalcedony","blocks":[{"text":"Chalcedony is an aggregate of cryptocrystalline or microcrystalline silica, or in simpler terms, a mass of individual silica crystals that are too small to be seen without magnification. It is composed primarily of quartz, with minor amounts of intergrown moganite. These are both silica minerals with a chemical composition of SiO2, but they differ in that quartz has a more symmetrical crystal structure (trigonal) than moganite (monoclinic). Mineralogically speaking, the quartz and moganite crystals in chalcedony must grow in parallel chains, called \"fibers.\" However, the term chalcedony may more broadly refer to any microcrystalline or cryptocrystalline silica, fibrous or not."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Quartz-46809.jpg/330px-Quartz-46809.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chalcedony","wikidata":"Q170448"},{"title":"Violet (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Violet is the color of light at the short wavelength end of the visible spectrum. It is one of the seven colors that Isaac Newton labeled when dividing the spectrum of visible light in 1704. Violet light has a wavelength between approximately 380 and 435 nanometers. The color's name is derived from the Viola genus of flowers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Amatista_Laye_2.jpg/330px-Amatista_Laye_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Violet_(color)","wikidata":"Q428124"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Mog%C3%A1nite-798422.jpg/500px-Mog%C3%A1nite-798422.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Mog%C3%A1nite-798422.jpg/500px-Mog%C3%A1nite-798422.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A rare pseudomorph of a spiral Turritella-like snail shell that has been replaced by chalcedony","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMog%C3%A1nite-798422.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Sumin_chalcedony_mouse.jpg/500px-Sumin_chalcedony_mouse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Sumin_chalcedony_mouse.jpg/1280px-Sumin_chalcedony_mouse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Sumin_chalcedony_mouse.jpg/500px-Sumin_chalcedony_mouse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chalcedony mouse, by Avenir Sumin","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASumin_chalcedony_mouse.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Quartz_-_Agateplate%2C_redbrown-white.jpg/500px-Quartz_-_Agateplate%2C_redbrown-white.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Quartz_-_Agateplate%2C_redbrown-white.jpg/1280px-Quartz_-_Agateplate%2C_redbrown-white.jpg?utm